{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 41, :comments ["<b>And it was in the morning, and Balak took, etc., and he saw, etc.</b> The saying of <b>וַיְהִי</b> is in the language of distress — perhaps because Balaam quarreled with Balak and distressed him with words over the meager quantity of meat he sent, as I have written. And the saying of <b>וַיִּקַּח בָּלָק</b> means he took him with words — meaning, he persuaded him about the matter." "Further, it may intend that since Balaam was lame in his leg, and according to the natural disposition habitual to those who sense the ride of human beings, a man such as this would not set his lame leg to walk in the morning, and all the more so to ascend the high places. And perhaps this is precisely what the verse intends with the word <b>וַיְהִי</b> — woe to Balaam! And the reason for the distress is because the matter took place in the morning. And since he could not walk immediately and at once, Balak would take him by his hand and support him to bring him up, and this is what it says: ״וַיִּקַּח בָּלָק אֶת בִּלְעָם וַיַּעֲלֵהוּ בָּמוֹת״, etc." "<b>Further,</b> the verse intends by saying <b>בַּבֹּקֶר</b> to inform you that the sleep of Balak's eyes fled, for it is the way of kings to remain in their beds until three hours, as they said of blessed memory (Berakhot 9a) — yet here, when morning came, he arose and took Balaam and brought him up to the high places, etc." "Another reason for <b>בַּבֹּקֶר</b>, according to what they said in tractate Avodah Zarah (Avodah Zarah 4a), and this is their language: ״At what time does it seethe? Abaye said: In the first three hours.״ Up to here. For this reason it says ״וַיְהִי בַּבֹּקֶר״, and it was precise to say <b>וַיְהִי</b> in the language of distress — meaning, a time when HaShem grows wrathful with His world, which is in the morning — he took Balaam, who was skilled in fixing the moment. Further, it may intend according to their saying of blessed memory (Numbers Rabbah 20:18), and this is their language: ״בָּמוֹת בַּעַל — this is Peor,״ up to here. And it is well known that Peor's mode of worship is the excretion of dung before it, and this is the language of their words of blessed memory (Sanhedrin 64a): ״With what does one worship the idolatry of Peor? One eats beets, drinks strong drink, and relieves oneself before it,״ up to here. You see that the worship of Peor is through the excretion of what is repugnant, and for this reason Balak wished to bring Balaam up to Peor, which is בָּמוֹת בַּעַל, at the time of worship — which is the time of relieving oneself, when a person attends to his needs. And this is what it says: <b>בַּבֹּקֶר וַיַּעֲלֵהוּ בָּמוֹת בָּעַל</b>. Or perhaps it is because Balaam did not practice this form of idolatry, so he took him with words to worship it." "Further, a reason for <b>בַּבֹּקֶר</b> appears according to their words of blessed memory (Zohar on this parashah), who said that Balak's sorceries were in a bird (צִפּוֹר), as we hinted in the discourse on ״בֶּן צִפּוֹר.״ For this reason Balak chose to stand in the morning, for then the bird chirps as is known, and through it his sorceries and divinations would rise in their hands, for that is its time." "<b>And with this</b> it appears possible to give a reason for what one may wonder at in this matter — in the verse's saying ״וַיַּרְא מִשָּׁם קְצֵה הָעָם״ — for did not our Sages of blessed memory say (Ta'anit 9a) that the clouds covered Israel and they were not visible to the eye, and when Aaron died and the clouds departed they became visible, and the Canaanite learned of their encampment and fought with them, and the clouds afterward returned through the merit of Moses, as they said of blessed memory (ibid.)? If so, Israel were covered by the cloud — so how could the verse say ״וַיַּרְא מִשָּׁם קְצֵה הָעָם״ — for on the contrary, HaShem should have added extra protection upon their existing protection, so that the evil eye of Balaam not enter among them! Rather, it is certain that what they saw of the edge of the people was by means of sorceries that they performed with the known bird, and it was what revealed them, for there is power in sorcery to do thus, as they said of blessed memory (Sanhedrin 67b): why is it called כְּשָׁפִים (sorcery) etc., and they — through sorcery — uncovered what was covered by the cloud. And the verse informs us that they uncovered for them only the edge of the people, not the corner of the righteous and their great ones."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:13:17.157447983Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}